  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.1. Increased capacity of the member States to ratify international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 7 May 2020 the International Labour Office has received the instrument of ratification of the Maritime Labour Convention, 2006, as amended (MLC, 2006) by Brazil, which became the 97th Member State of the ILO to have ratified the Convention. Brazil is the seventh Latin American country to commit to this landmark instrument. 
Brazil is a key maritime player with a merchant fleet of more than 3 million gross tonnage and a coastline measuring almost 7,500 kilometres, including several very important ports. Brazil had already ratified 13 maritime Conventions, which were revised by the MLC, 2006. Through adhering to the most updated ILO maritime labour instrument, the MLC, 2006, Brazil renews its commitment to the protection of the rights of seafarers and a level playing field for shipowners.
 Brazilian constituents have been very involved in the development of the MLC ever since the establishment of the Subgroup of the High-Level Tripartite Working Group on Maritime Labour Standards in 2002. Following the adoption of the MLC in 2006, ILO provided advisory and kept direct contacts with Brazilian authorities during which ILO officials met government officials to help address issues in the application of the MLC standards, including in the form of an Awareness-raising workshop on the MLC, 2006 for seafarers and seafarers&apos; representative held in Rio de Janeiro (March 2016)</narrative>
   </description>
